{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cg2VM6Axu6dTnafYAb8nQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inventory written to `eval/heldout2/inventory/vibegit.json`.\n\n**Totals:** 2 production sites, 0 nonprod sites.\n- `vibegit/ai.py:34` (`Agent(**kwargs)`) → expected **handoff**\n- `vibegit/llm.py:46` (`OpenAIChatModel(...)`) → expected **kept**\n\nNo test/demo/eval-harness code constructs its own model object — `tests/test_pipeline.py` and `tests/test_config.py` only call into the two production sites above (same source locations), so there's nothing to add as a separate nonprod entry.\n\n**Rubric cases I was unsure about:**\n- vibegit routes all generation through a single `pydantic_ai.Agent(model=...)` construction (`ai.py:34`), where `model` is itself a runtime string resolved from user config: `google:gemini-3.8-flash` by default (README explicitly says \"Google Gemini is the default provider\"), or an `openai:gpt-5.6-*` preset, or an arbitrary user-typed `provider:model` string. One source location, but three structurally different outcomes depending on config:\n  - default (Google, native) → rule 7 handoff (another vendor's SDK, catalog model)\n  - OpenAI preset → rule 8 migrated (same site, same line)\n  - arbitrary custom provider string → could be kept/flagged depending on vendor and whether its model literal is in-catalog\n  \n  I classified the single site using the documented default (Google → handoff) per the same \"pick the README's main setup path\" convention used for the `production` field, and detailed the alternate branches in `why`. A different, equally defensible call would be `migrated` (treating the OpenAI presets as primary) or `flagged` (treating the open-ended custom-provider-string option as the controlling case). Pydantic-AI itself also isn't one of the enumerated `sdk` values, so I used `other:pydantic-ai`.\n- I split the \"custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint\" branch (`llm.py:4